    },
    {
      "text": "Like swe bench is all in Python, we're Ruby on Rails. It is not the case that the benchmarks are identical for them.",
      "timestamp_sec": 817,
      "why": "Names the specific reason public benchmarks may not transfer to a given team."
    },
    {
      "text": "you can see that the Anthropic agents have just been consistently getting better, but not really any faster. The Codex agents and cursor are actually pretty fast and quite good.",
      "timestamp_sec": 846,
      "why": "A rare public head-to-head read on agent harnesses from real internal data."
    },
    {
      "text": "By cost, the Anthropic stuff is clearly just so much more expensive for us.",
      "timestamp_sec": 846,
      "why": "The cost finding that changed their default harness."
    },
    {
      "text": "essentially 100% like 99.9% of our pull requests are like heavily agent generated. We know that quality and reliability and security are really important, so we still have humans look at everything.",
      "timestamp_sec": 947,
      "why": "Pairs an extreme adoption number with an explicit human-review guardrail."
    },
    {
      "text": "we had 3,300 Claude code runs that cost $10,000 in tokens daily. We have plans, so we didn't spend $10,000 on it. And Codex had four times as many sessions, and it was cheaper overall.",
      "timestamp_sec": 980,
      "why": "The hardest numbers in the talk, including the honest caveat about subscription pricing."
    },
    {
      "text": "And lastly, find a way to benchmark and become model agnostic so you're not tied to anybody and you can just constantly stay at that right part on the frontier of cost, speed, quality.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1061,
      "why": "Closing recommendation that ties benchmarking to the agnosticism thesis."
    }
